    @section mongodb_generic_cursor Typed Cursor Example

    <tt>MongoCursor&lt;DocT&gt;</tt> is generic. The builtin collection
    APIs return <tt>MongoCursor&lt;hash&lt;auto&gt;&gt;</tt>, which makes the document
    type visible to the parser, reflection, API metadata, and generated docs:

    @code{.py}
%requires mongodb

MongoClient client("mongodb://localhost:27017");
MongoDatabase db = client.getDatabase("example");
MongoCollection users = db.getCollection("users");

MongoCursor<hash<auto>> cursor = users.find({"status": "active"});
while (*hash<auto> doc = cursor.next()) {
    printf("User: %y\n", doc);
}

list<hash<auto>> docs = users.aggregate((
    {"$match": {"status": "active"}},
    {"$project": {"name": 1, "email": 1}},
)).toList();
    @endcode

    @section mongodbconnection Connection Strings

    MongoDB connections use the standard MongoDB connection string format:
    @code{.py}
    mongodb://[username:password@]host[:port][/database][?options]
    @endcode

    Examples:
    @code{.py}
    # Connect to local MongoDB
    MongoClient client("mongodb://localhost:27017");

    # Connect with authentication
    MongoClient client("mongodb://user:password@localhost:27017/mydb?authSource=admin");

    # Connect to replica set
    MongoClient client("mongodb://host1:27017,host2:27017,host3:27017/?replicaSet=myReplicaSet");
    @endcode

    @section mongodbsandbox Sandboxed Code

    Each connection that the module makes for code that a sandbox governs is checked against the sandbox's network
    policy, for every address that it connects to; the sandbox of the program that created the client applies as
    well as the sandbox of the program that uses it.  The driver also connects to other hosts by itself for some
    options, through no connection that the module makes, so sandboxed code is refused them:
    - the authentication mechanisms for which the driver fetches credentials or tokens from the environment's
      endpoints itself (\c MONGODB-AWS without credentials in the connection string, and \c MONGODB-OIDC with an
      \c ENVIRONMENT) are refused with \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED when sandboxed code creates the client, and when
      sandboxed code connects with such a client
    - the TLS connections of sandboxed code never contact an OCSP responder: a stapled OCSP response is still
      checked (as with the \c tlsDisableOCSPEndpointCheck option)
    - \c GSSAPI authentication, for which Kerberos contacts the KDCs itself, is allowed to sandboxed code only when its
      network policy is unrestricted (as for the krb5 module): it is refused with \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED otherwise,
      when the client is created and when it connects

